<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guayaquil Conference</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1822">1822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> Conference was the meeting of the two leading liberators of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a>, at Guayaquil on 26 and 27 July 1822. Held in private, it left no reliable record, but soon afterwards San Martín withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and public life, leaving Bolívar to complete the liberation of Peru.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guayaquil-conference.jpg" alt="Guayaquil Conference" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Padaguan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detalle_del_Hemiciclo_de_la_Rotonda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> and the most important city of the Spanish colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, was a royalist stronghold in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence">Spanish American wars of independence</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilean-war-of-independence">Chilean War of Independence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> organised a navy that let his forces besiege and take the city, and he declared the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, though a strong royalist force remained in the Peruvian countryside. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>, meanwhile, had liberated Venezuela and, with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a>, the United Provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, creating Gran Colombia.</p><p>A revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>, carried out by the city's garrison, the Cuzco Reserve Grenadiers regiment of Peruvian royalists, freed the city from Spanish rule in 1820, and other Ecuadorian cities followed. Some factions wished to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, others <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and others to form a new nation. Bolívar settled the question by annexing Guayaquil to Colombia, while San Martín was under Peruvian pressure to claim it for Peru.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>San Martín arrived on 25 July and was warmly greeted by Bolívar. The two were to decide how the war should end while the royalists regrouped, how the new countries could secure their independence, and what should become of the Free Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>. Despite common goals and mutual respect, and even though San Martín offered to serve under Bolívar, they could not agree. Bolívar favoured republics, drawing on his own version of the theory behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a>; San Martín preferred European, especially British, models and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchies</a>, with a European prince as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After several hours of talks on 27 July, San Martín stayed for a banquet and ball in his honour. Bolívar raised a toast to San Martín and himself as the two foremost men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>; San Martín drank to the prompt end of the war, the organisation of the republics of the continent and the health of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">the Liberator</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>. He then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, laid down his powers before the new Peruvian Congress, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and later retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The meeting is considered a turning point of South American independence, and it inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-luis-borges">Jorge Luis Borges</a>'s story <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>, published in El informe de Brodie in 1971.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guayaquil Conference” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Days later Bolívar met San Martín in the <b>Guayaquil Conference</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-province-of-guayaquil">Free Province of Guayaquil</a></li></ul></section>
